From: dragonleopardpig <dragonleopardpig@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] hyprland: stop re-arming socket reader after EOF

`watch_socket` re-arms `read_line_async` unconditionally after each
read, even when the previous read returned `null` (EOF / closed stream).
The generated C wrapper for `handle_event(string line)` adds a
`g_return_if_fail(line != NULL)` at function entry, so the null line is
caught -- but only after the warning is logged, and the outer reader
has already scheduled itself again on the dead stream. The result is a
hot loop emitting

  astal_hyprland_hyprland_handle_event: assertion 'line != NULL' failed

at line-rate (observed: 37k/s), which floods the journal and starves
the gjs main loop. Side effects in HyprPanel include every
StatusNotifierItem's right-click context menu becoming unresponsive
until the panel is restarted.

Triggered in practice when a chatty Hyprland client (e.g. an app with
several short-lived sub-windows) shuts down and the event socket
transiently returns EOF.

Bail out on the null read. A backoff/reconnect loop would be a nicer
follow-up.
---
diff --git a/src/hyprland.vala b/src/hyprland.vala
--- a/src/hyprland.vala
+++ b/src/hyprland.vala
@@ -138,6 +138,12 @@ public class Hyprland : Object {
         stream.read_line_async.begin(Priority.DEFAULT, null, (_, res) => {
                 try {
                     var line = stream.read_line_async.end(res);
+                    if (line == null) {
+                        // EOF / closed stream: don't re-arm or we hot-loop,
+                        // tripping g_return_if_fail in handle_event forever.
+                        critical("Hyprland event socket closed");
+                        return;
+                    }
                     handle_event.begin(line, (_, res) => {
                         try {
                             handle_event.end(res);
